he query forces two scans, and I think both are likely causes. I only have the one-line `query.sql`. There's no schema, no `EXPLAIN` output and no migration history in the repo, so this is from reading the SQL.\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Sequential scan on `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database has to read every user row and lowercase each email. This one is certain unless an expression index on `lower(email)` already exists.\n2. **Sequential scan on `orders`, or a hash join that reads all of it.** The join on `o.user_id` is only cheap if `orders.user_id` is indexed. Without that index, the database reads the whole `orders` table to find one user's rows. This is the bigger cost if `orders` is large, and I can't tell from here whether the index exists.\n3. **Wide row fetch.** `SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables, including duplicate `id` and `created_at` columns. That adds I/O and prevents index-only scans.\n\n**Fixes**\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Make the lookup indexable (unique if emails are unique case-insensitively)\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_lower_email ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- 2. Make the join indexable\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nThen name the columns the page needs instead of `*`:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, o.status\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower('a@example.org');\n```\n\nIf you'd rather not index an expression, you could store emails lowercased, or use `citext` for the column. Then the filter becomes `u.email = 'a@example.org'` with a normal index. Either way, the application and the index must both apply `lower()`, or the index won't be used.\n\nThe syntax above is Postgres. For MySQL, drop `CONCURRENTLY`. A functional index needs MySQL 8.0.13 or later, and a case-insensitive collation already makes `lower()` unnecessary.\n\n**Why it slowed down last week**\n\nThe query text doesn't explain a sudden jump. Something else changed. The usual suspects are:\n- `orders` or `users` crossed a size where the planner switched plans.\n- An index was dropped, or a migration didn't apply.\n- The ORM or app code started wrapping the email in `lower()`.\n- The page started running this query more than once.\n\nRun `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the query to confirm. Look for `Seq Scan on users` and `Seq Scan on orders`. If you paste the plan and the table definitions, I can say which fix matters most. I can also check how the app calls this query if you point me to the code.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}